2026-06-30 15:44:23,822 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-d08838f7-82cd-4c38-9ee4-0f1cab2c17d4', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n20200206_PC689-Moorman_1.raw\n20200206_PC689-Moorman_2.raw\n20200206_PC689-Moorman_3.raw\n20200206_PC689-Moorman_4.raw\n20200206_PC689-Moorman_5.raw\n20200206_PC689-Moorman_6.raw\n20200206_PC689-Moorman_7.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nIdentification of interactors with HCMV pTRS1 using BioID and proteomics\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nHuman cytomegalovirus (HCMV) is a ubiquitous pathogen that infects the majority of the world’s population. Lytic HCMV replication in immunocompromised individuals or neonates can lead to severe disease in multiple organ systems and even death. The establishment of lytic replication is driven by the first viral proteins expressed upon infection, the immediate early proteins, which play a key role in creating an intracellular environment conducive to virus replication. Two immediate early proteins, the functional orthologs pTRS1 and pIRS1, stimulate immediate early gene expression by suppressing antiviral PKR/eIF2a signaling and enhance the translation of viral mRNAs independent of PKR antagonism. To better understand the molecular functions of pTRS1, we used proximity labeling proteomics to identify proteins that interact with pTRS1 in infected cells. Multiple novel host and viral interactors were identified, including the catalytic subunits of the protein phosphatase 1 (PP1) holoenzyme. Mutations to a PP1 catalytic subunit known to disrupt binding to PP1 regulatory subunits decreased binding to pTRS1. pTRS1 immune complexes contained phosphatase activity, and inhibition of phosphatase activity in transfected or infected cells reversed the ability of pTRS1 to inhibit  the antiviral kinase PKR. Depletion of individual PP1 catalytic subunits decreased virus replication and increased the phosphorylation of the PKR substrate eIF2a. Together our data suggest potential novel functions for pTRS1, and define a novel role for PP1 as an antagonist of the antiviral PKR/eIF2\uf061 signaling axis during HCMV infection.\n\n[METHODS]\nMRC5 cells were mock infected or infected with AdTRS1BioID (MOI of 3) and incubated in growth medium with 50 µM biotin at 37℃ for 24 hours prior to harvest at 48 hours after infection. Cells were lysed in RIPA buffer containing urea (50 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.4, 150 mM NaCl, 1% NP-40, 0.25% deoxycholate, 1 mM EDTA and 2M urea) on ice for fifteen minutes and insoluble material was removed by centrifugation at 15000 rpm for ten minutes at 4oC. Equal protein amounts of each lysate, as determined by the Bradford assay, were mixed with Streptavidin T1 Dynabeads (Thermo) and nutated for three hours at 4oC. Beads were washed three times in RIPA buffer containing urea followed by three washes with 25 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.8 prior to being resuspended in 25 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.8. On-bead trypsin digestion was performed followed by desalting using C18 columns (Pierce). Proteomic profiling was performed by liquid chromatography tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) using an Easy nLC 1200 coupled to a QExactive HF mass spectrometer (Thermo).\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
